utcome, and add a negative constraint clarifying that this doesn't apply to CompositeMention or SpecificDisease cases, which follow their own definitions. I'll finalize this as one bullet with a nested negative-constraint sub-bullet, keeping it tight and outputting only the list as instructed.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Specificity Determines the Label, Not the Syntactic Frame:** IF a disease mention is a bare, unspecified superordinate category term (a general class noun with no discriminating premodifier, proper or eponymous name, or acronym), THEN label it **DiseaseClass**, even when it is the complement of a prepositional phrase governed by a relational or possessive noun (e.g., \"[noun] of ___\"). IF a disease string is a named or specified disease (it carries a discriminating specifier, a proper name, or an acronym) and functions as a noun-noun premodifier of a non-disease head, THEN label it **Modifier**, and keep that label when the same specified string appears in a relational complement frame.\n  - **Negative constraint:** Do not treat the relational frame or the \"of\"-complement position as the deciding cue in either direction. This rule does not apply to disease spans that form part of a coordinated or composite mention (**CompositeMention**) or to a disease named as the focal subject of the study (**SpecificDisease**); those cases follow their own entity definitions.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1589, "output_tokens": 2993, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 2642}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}